{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"fervid-boring-python","creation_channel":"cli","description":"## Summary\n\nThe `Reconcile Bridge` \"Commit reconciler events back and push to origin/tickets\"\nstep exhausts its 5 retries and fails the workflow red under normal `tickets`-branch\ncontention. Spun out of bug `449f-f9bf-be90-47fe` (mode 2), whose fix covered the\nother two failure modes; this one is separated because it changes retry semantics of\na shared push path and needs a contention harness to test honestly.\n\nTwo distinct defects, plus a disposition question.\n\n## Defect 1 — the backoff is anti-adaptive for this failure class\n\n`.github/workflows/reconcile-bridge.yml:328`:\n\n```sh\nbackoff=$(( (1 << attempt) + RANDOM % 3 ))\n```\n\nBy the time this sleep runs, the loop has **already** fetched and merged\n`origin/tickets` (`:321`), so its HEAD is a valid fast-forward *at that instant*. The\nonly thing that can invalidate it is another push landing first. Sleeping therefore\nmonotonically increases the probability of losing the very race the retry exists to\nwin — and the sleep **grows** (3 → 6 → 10 → 17s) exactly as the remaining attempt\nbudget shrinks.\n\nExponential backoff is correct for load-shedding against an overloaded or\nrate-limited server. It is the wrong algorithm for an optimistic-concurrency CAS on a\nhot append-only ref, where the correct move after a successful merge is to re-push\n**immediately**.\n\n## Evidence — full retry timeline recovered from run 30585738860\n\nEvery one of the five rejections is preceded by a *successful* merge and a\n*successful* remote-tracking advance. The merges converge; only the push loses.\n\n```\n22:13:23.7  pre-loop fetch                      60ccc6506..4d37b6925\n22:13:24.2  attempt 1 rejected (non-fast-forward)\n22:13:24.7  merge OK (ort), \"Waiting 3s\"\n22:13:28.3  attempt 2 rejected                  4d37b6925..dd70be0d7\n22:13:29.0  merge OK, \"Waiting 6s\"\n22:13:35.5  attempt 3 rejected                  dd70be0d7..a46013f4c\n22:13:36.2  merge OK, \"Waiting 10s\"\n22:13:46.7  attempt 4 rejected                  a46013f4c..f3a1fe61f\n22:13:47.5  merge OK, \"Waiting 17s\"\n22:14:05.0  attempt 5 rejected                  f3a1fe61f..939e92676\n22:14:05.7  merge OK — never pushed, loop exits, exit 1\n```\n\nDerived: five remote advances over 41.9s => mean competitor interval **~8.4s**.\nBackoff budget 30-38s of a ~42s loop. Each remote advance lands *inside* the\ncorresponding sleep.\n\nModelled with Poisson competitors at lambda = 1/8.4s, the per-attempt loss\nprobabilities are 35% / 54% / 72% / 88%, giving **P(all five fail) ~= 12% per run**.\nWith no sleep (window ~0.6s) the same model gives **~1.7e-6**.\n\nObserved rate is consistent: 4 failures on 2026-07-30 clustered in contention bursts.\n\n## Defect 2 — the final merge is computed and then discarded\n\n`.github/workflows/reconcile-bridge.yml:296-332`: the loop body is\n`push -> classify -> fetch -> merge -> (if attempt < max) sleep`. On attempt 5 it\nperforms a full fetch+merge and then falls out of the `while` **without ever pushing\nthat merge**. The log above shows it: `f3a1fe61f..939e92676` is merged at 22:14:05.7\nand the next line is `Push failed after 5 retries`.\n\nThat discarded merge had, by construction, just produced a fast-forwardable HEAD — a\nfree ~93%-likely attempt thrown away. Five pushes cost five merges and used four.\n\n## The authority this violates\n\nThe workflow states its own intent at `:315-320`: the merge-not-rebase choice\n\"mirrors the local client path (`_store/push.py`) and invariant I1\n(`docs/concurrency.md`)\". The path it claims to mirror has **no backoff at all** —\n`src/rebar/_store/push.py:167-210` re-pushes immediately on the next iteration after\n`merge.returncode == 0 -> continue`. The workflow deviates from its stated reference\nimplementation in precisely the dimension that determines convergence.\n\n## Open question — is red even the right disposition?\n\n`docs/concurrency.md:297-300` states the contract for this operation: \"Push is\n**best-effort**: a failed push (no network, unresolvable non-fast-forward, recovery\nstate) **never fails the caller** — it warns, leaves local commits intact, and the\nbranch stays diverged. `rebar fsck` surfaces that divergence as a `PUSH_PENDING`\nnotice ... so it is not silent.\" `push.py:265` and `:286-291` implement exactly that:\nwarn, return, raise nothing.\n\nThe workflow instead exits 1. It has no `PUSH_PENDING` equivalent, so the missing\nartifact may be the notice channel rather than the severity.\n\nCounter-consideration that must be settled first, NOT assumed: the runner is\nephemeral, so a lost push discards the merged local commits with the container, while\nthe Jira-side mutations were already applied. Whether that is benign depends on\nwhether the next pass re-derives the same events. `docs/jira-sync-setup.md:151-154`\nsuggests it does (\"a pass that times out makes no durable progress and the next pass\nre-does the work\"), but that is about a timed-out pass, not a lost push.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] Decide and record whether an exhausted push race should be red or a\n      `PUSH_PENDING`-style non-red notice, resolving it against\n      `docs/concurrency.md:297-300` rather than by preference. Evidence required:\n      confirm for the 4 failed runs (30503669644, 30510284129, 30515516036,\n      30585738860) that the events they failed to push were re-derived by a later\n      pass, and that no duplicate Jira issue was created for the same `local_id`.\n- [ ] Retry-after-successful-merge no longer sleeps an increasing interval; the\n      re-push after a clean merge is immediate (matching `_store/push.py`).\n      Any retained delay is justified in a comment against a named failure mode.\n- [ ] The final merge is either pushed or not computed — no iteration performs a\n      merge whose result is provably discarded.\n- [ ] A deterministic contention harness covers this: a scripted competitor that\n      pushes before each attempt, asserting the loop converges rather than\n      exhausting. RED against the current loop first.\n- [ ] Measured: over >= 500 subsequent `Reconcile Bridge` runs, zero failures\n      attributable to push-race exhaustion (`gh run list` evidence).\n- [ ] File paths: `.github/workflows/reconcile-bridge.yml` (lines 294-335);\n      `src/rebar/_store/push.py` if the budgets are reconciled (it uses 3, the\n      workflow 5).","id":"4c4f-e554-849a-4dfd","parent_id":"","priority":2,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"bug","title":"Reconcile Bridge push retry: exponential backoff is anti-adaptive for a CAS race, and the final merge is computed then discarded"},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1785454512113349001,"uuid":"35c0454a-65a0-4bb0-a32f-497dc0c28057"}